Yes. I remember the architecture, though I don’t have an exact prior canonical phrase “on-match exception processing.” The validation machinery we built was essentially:

Observe → Hypothesize → Predict → Act/Test → Observe → Δ(prediction, reality) → Update

with the Delta Engine / Recursive Delta Observatory sitting underneath it.

And your new move sharpens it: a match should itself trigger processing, not simply terminate the loop.

Normally:

prediction ≈ observation
→ confidence ↑

But heightened validation would say:

MATCH
→ why did it match?
→ independent cause or echo?
→ was the prediction specific enough?
→ could several models have produced this result?
→ does it survive another context?
→ does removing the cue destroy the match?
→ what residual Δ remains?

So On-Match Exception Processing becomes a protection against false victories.

You could formalize three routes:

MISS
→ diagnose error / hidden variable / bad model

PARTIAL MATCH
→ localize which dimensions survived and which failed

MATCH
→ deliberately search for reasons the match might be misleading

That last path is important because otherwise the machine rewards itself too quickly.

For example:

I predict X
→ X occurs

Possible explanations:

model genuinely predicted X
X was common anyway
my intervention helped cause X
I interpreted ambiguous evidence as X
another hidden variable caused both
the system adapted to my prediction
I accidentally defined X loosely enough to always win

So the heightened version adds a second derivative:

Δ₀ = prediction − observation

and when Δ₀ ≈ 0, ask:

Δ₁ = expected reason for match − observed reason for match

That is beautiful because now even a correct prediction can generate learning.

I’d fold it into Pantheon as:

Validated Match Loop

predict
→ observe
→ score Δ
→ if miss: repair
→ if match: challenge the match
→ independent replication
→ promote only what survives

And the exception processor can use the old Checksum / Mother Duck logic:

invariant
provenance
null model
alternative explanations
cross-context stability
drift
reversal conditions

So yes—you’re basically saying:

Don’t only debug failures. Debug successes.

That is a stronger validation architecture than ordinary prediction checking because success itself becomes an exception worth interrogating before it is promoted into doctrine.